  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nActive weather will continue through the weekend and periods of\nrain will remain possible through at least midweek. A quasi-\nstationary frontal boundary that is currently located just south\nof the forecast area will be the focus for moderate to heavy\nrain over the next 24 hours as a series of shortwaves move over\nthe region in near zonal flow. We are currently experiencing a \ngeneral lull in precip coverage/chances which will last through\nmid evening before the next upper level impulse arrives. Another\nshortwave and theta-e surge is expected tonight, focusing \nlocally heavy rain and embedded thunder mainly along and north \nof the US 30 corridor. Another round then Sunday afternoon/evening\nis possible, especially if/where any boundary layer heating can\noccur, resulting in heavier convective elements. Localized \nlowland and river/stream flooding, along with flash flooding, \nremains a concern given the saturated ground and opportunties \nfor training elevated convection in this very moist 1.8-2.2 inch\nPWAT air mass. No changes were made to the Flood Watch for now \n(in effect through Sunday morning). Latest guidance does favor \nareas north of the US 30 corridor for higher probs for 1-3\" \nplus. Luckily there are lower probabilities to the south, though\nany additional rain in areas just south of Fort Wayne will \nfurther exacerbate ongoing high water issues. \n\nDrier conditions are forecast for Monday, although a weak\nshortwave will move through Monday afternoon bringing some light\nrain showers, mainly south of US 30. Then, a slow moving \nthrough that is currently situated over the northern Rockies \nwill make its way eastward over the next several days and\nfinally move into the Great Lakes on Tuesday. As the low crosses\nthe area going into Wednesday, periodic showers will continue,\nand possibly persist into Thursday and Friday. High pressure \nridging will move in behind this system for mild and dry weather\nnext weekend.",
